  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 220 AM EDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nThe beginning of the week will feature the rather deep mid level \ntrough still remaining over the Southeast as well as the northern \nGulf on Monday. However, the latest global and ensemble guidance \nsuite does show mid level ridging in the western Atlantic trying \nto push back over the region starting later on Monday and then \neven more so heading into Tuesday and Wednesday. At the surface, \nwhat is left of the second frontal boundary stalled out over \nNorthern Florida as well as the Northern Gulf on Monday will \ngradually wash out heading into Tuesday. The western periphery of\nsurface high pressure centered in the western Atlantic will then \ngradually shift back over the Florida Peninsula for Tuesday and \nWednesday. This will create a wind shift allowing for winds to \nbecome south southeasterly as Monday progresses, and then slowly \nbecoming easterly moving towards the middle of the week.\n\nWith the influx of moisture continuing especially through monday, \ncombined with the light south to southwest steering flow in\nplace, scattered to numerous showers and thunderstorms will \ndevelop as the sea breezes push inland. The east coast sea breeze\nwill still remain relatively pinned for one more day on Monday, \nallowing for the highest chances of convection to remain over the \ninterior as well as the east coast metro areas. With PWAT values \ngenerally looking to remain at 2.0 inches or higher during this \ntime frame, heavy downpours and slow moving thunderstorms could \ncontinue the potential for localized flooding on Monday. While \nTuesday and Wednesday will feature additional shower and \nthunderstorm development, the highest chances will shift and \nremain over the interior and southwest Florida each afternoon and \nevening during this time frame. High temperatures each day through\nthe middle of the week will continue to rise into the lower 90s \nacross most of South Florida. Peak heat index values will still \ncreate a heat concern during this time frame as they will range \nbetween 105 and 110 each afternoon. \n\nEasterly winds remain firmly in place heading into the second half \nof the week, however, uncertainty rises in the forecast during this \ntime frame as some of the global and ensemble guidance is hinting at \nthe potential for another weak TUTT to make a close pass near the \nregion. This could increase the chances of showers and thunderstorms \nduring this time frame and it will continue to be monitored as \nthe week progresses. With easterly wind flow remaining in place, \nthe highest chances of showers and thunderstorms during this time \nframe will continue to be focused over the interior as well as \nSouthwest Florida.",
